A||||3||Neal||Wrong Torque Spec for 88 Brakes||||8||
Z||000000||Neal||06-06-2001||02:06 AM||||Well ive got some advice here DONT follow the torque specs in the haynes manual for the brake hose to caliper bolt on an 88 fiero.<BR>It gives 33 ft pounds. DONT go as high as 33 ft pounds<BR>33 ft pounds easily striped out the threads.  And the worst part about it is that its not my car.  I was helping a friend replace his brake pads and the caliper had to be removed because the piston came out.  Reninstall everything and it all goes real smooth untill... the bolt stripes.<P>So we need to get the car on the road ASAP shoud we get a new caliper or get that one heli-coild?||||reg||8
Z||000001||84Bill||06-06-2001||07:35 AM||||I wouldnt mess with helicoiling a fluid line. IMO get another caliper and be safe||||reg||14
Z||000002||FieroJoe||06-06-2001||10:59 AM||||This past sunday someone came into Ace where I work looking for a helicoil set.  I dug up an old dusty display from the basement with a few hanging on it, and they were like $50 for a set with the tools to set them.  I dont think I would trust anything like that, not to mention that they were expensive.<P>Anyways, dont you still have to drill out part of your damaged threads before you use the heli coil?  You'll still get shavings in the caliper, so its better just to get a new one.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||06-06-2001||10:59 AM||||Bill, thats what i was thinking, also easier to get a new one ordered than messing around with finding and installing the heli-coil<P>Checked the chiltons manual, they also spec 33 ft pounds.  I think to be safe ill use the tq spec for an 86/87 which is like 15 ft #.||||reg||1
